            "text": "Midnight arrives quietly in the city, though nothing actually stops. Streetlights hover like tentative suns, drawing wavering constellations on wet pavement. A delivery cyclist arcs through the glow, plastic crate rattling a rhythm older than commerce, echoing the ancient barter of hunger and motion. From a window above, someone practicing scales on a muted trumpet pushes the air into reluctant shimmer. Each note stalls, then resolves, apologizing for being awake so late. I like to imagine that buildings sigh during these hours, bricks loosening their squared shoulders, elevators breathing out after hauling thousands of vertical miles. The city\u2019s memory feels longest at night, listing through events the way a tired reader leafs absently toward the last page. It remembers fires and festivals, marriages sworn under new neon, defeats carried home in soft paper bags. By noon, all this tenderness will harden again into angles: honking, argument, ambition. But right now, while midnight lingers, nobody owns the avenues. They belong to hush, the sodium candlelight, the tamped heartbeat of people not yet asleep, and the drifting curiosity of people not yet awake. Somewhere, bread is rising, yeast conversing with flour about morning. Somewhere, a stray dog inventories stars, confident its report will be filed with dawn. And I, sitting beside a half-finished sentence, wonder whether language ever quite catches midnight, or whether it merely chases the reflection of its own desire along the streets, never arriving, always discovering itself a syllable late, a breath behind, listening for the next hush again."
          }
